# DEC-260, batch 1 of 4: the member-facing English still written inline in Civicsmith's other modules, keyed (Bob through BOB,
# K3366: a module reads its words by key through setup-words' word(key, vars) and holds no English; DEC-258's model).
# This batch's 44 modules: skilldoctrine, setup, duties, strength, basis-versions, events, acquisition, filing-templates,
# ratification, capture, record-grammar, sign-release, idspaces, case-inputs, entities, record-core, review, image-cover,
# extraction, explore, capture-sources, lines, doorbell, doc-clean, case-tensions, inquiry-grammar, project-roster,
# subresources, queue, cdx, case-catalogue, case-check, cpu, livefire, container, deliverer, docx, formats, gate, inband,
# scheduler, screens-wizards, sshsig, tsa.
# What it keys: refusal translations by their code (refusal.<CODE>, the design's existing words governing where a code is
# already keyed); undetermined reasons, notes, statements and labels members read in answers, as <module>.<field>.<slug>;
# record-grammar's proposal and citable-passage provenance labels; skilldoctrine's six intake questions (investigation's by-
# hand intake); the signing-keys page (sign-release.html) whole, its static text included. Spelling is American, internal
# codes are dropped from member text, template variables are {name}. What stays unkeyed, each with its reason (refusal
# details, methods, diagnostics, the assistant's skill doctrine, the project file's own grammar), is listed in words-reads-
# mod-1.json beside each module's reads.
# Run: python3 words-mod-1.src.py, then node build_words.mjs.
import json, os
W = []
def w(key, en, prot=False, note=''):
    W.append(dict(key=key, en=en, protected=prot, note='DEC-260' + (': ' + note if note else '')))

